Add a `--status` filter to `list` rather than a whole new command.

```go
func cmdList(args []string) error {
	fs := flag.NewFlagSet("list", flag.ExitOnError)
	status := fs.String("status", "", "reading|finished")
	if err := fs.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	path, err := dataPath()
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	store, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}

	for _, b := range store.Books {
		if *status != "" && string(b.Status) != *status {
			continue
		}
		fmt.Printf("%d  %s  %s  %s  p.%d\n", b.ID, b.Title, b.Author, b.Status, b.Page)
	}
	return nil
}
```

Usage:

```
books list --status reading
books list --status finished
```

Same flag-before-args rule as before, but here there are no positional args, so it’s always safe.

If you’d rather have a dedicated verb:

```go
case "unfinished":
	err = cmdList([]string{"--status", "reading"})
```

then `books unfinished` works too. For a personal tool I’d keep the filter and skip the alias until you catch yourself typing it.
